meta {
  name: Undo Decision - not host
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/sessions/{{sessionId}}/requests/{{playerId}}
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{idToken2}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "status": "PENDING"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 403
  res.body.success: eq false
  res.body.error.code: eq FORBIDDEN
}

docs {
  Depends on **Reject Request** having run -- run as the **player** (`{{idToken2}}`)
  instead of the host, undoing their *own* rejected request. Mirrors **List Session
  Requests (not host)**: `UndoDecisionUseCase` checks `userId !== session.host.id` and
  throws `ForbiddenError` before ever looking at the request's own status, so this fails
  the same way regardless of whether the request is actually undoable -- being the host is
  checked first, being in a reversible state second.

  The request stays `REJECTED` after this -- a failed authorization check shouldn't have
  side effects. **Undo Rejection** (run as the host) is the one that actually reverts it.
}
